# Team Org Chart Builder

A single-file web app that draws an interactive organization chart: a top
position, departments, teams, and members assigned to those teams. Optionally
keep your roster in Excel and convert it with a small Python script.

> All names, teams and departments in this repository are **fictional sample data**.

## Features

- **Interactive chart**: click to rename the top box, departments and teams; add or delete departments, teams and members
- **Multi-team members**: one person can sit on several teams; their other teams show beside their name
- **Inactive members** shown in red, with a one-click toggle to hide them
- **Live search** that highlights matching teams and people
- **Auto-save** in the browser, **Import / Export** as JSON, **Print / Save as PDF**
- **Light and dark mode**, responsive layout, no dependencies, no server

## Quick start

Open `index.html` in any modern browser. That's it.

## Use your own data from Excel (optional)

1. Edit `Team_Roster_Sample.xlsx` (or a copy). It has four data sheets:

   | Sheet | Columns |
   |---|---|
   | Departments | Department, Lead |
   | Teams | Department, Team |
   | Members | Name, Status (Active / Inactive) |
   | Assignments | Member, Team (one row per membership) |

2. Convert it:

   ```bash
   pip install -r requirements.txt
   python build_data.py my_roster.xlsx -o org_data.json --title "My Org Chart" --top "CEO"
   ```

3. In `index.html`, click **Import data** and choose `org_data.json`.

`build_data.py` options: `workbook` (positional), `-o/--output`, `--title`, `--top`.
It prints a warning for any assignment that points to an unknown team or member.

## JSON format

```json
{
  "title": "Sample Company Team Hierarchy",
  "top": "Director",
  "members": [{ "name": "Alex Carter", "active": true }],
  "departments": [
    { "name": "Engineering", "lead": "Jordan Lee",
      "teams": [{ "name": "Platform Team", "members": ["Alex Carter"] }] }
  ]
}
```

## Project files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `index.html` | The whole app (HTML + CSS + JavaScript) |
| `build_data.py` | Excel roster to JSON converter |
| `Team_Roster_Sample.xlsx` | Example roster with dropdowns and count formulas |
| `org_data_sample.json` | Example output of the converter |
| `How_to_Use.pdf` | Short illustrated user guide |
| `requirements.txt` | Python dependency (`openpyxl`) |
| `.gitignore` | Keeps real rosters out of version control |

## Privacy

The app runs entirely in your browser and stores data only in that browser's
local storage. Nothing is uploaded. If you use real people's data, keep it out
of public repositories - the included `.gitignore` blocks every `.xlsx` and
`org_data.json` except the sample files.

## Tech

HTML, CSS, vanilla JavaScript; Python 3 with [openpyxl](https://openpyxl.readthedocs.io/).

